WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.932
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.232 --> 00:00:14.717
Unit five: Political Systems. Today you will evaluate past decisions with modal perfects.

4
00:00:15.017 --> 00:00:17.588
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:21.917
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:23.970
Democracy.

7
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:28.144
Democracy means government in which power rests with the people.

8
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.585
Democracy requires more than holding elections.

9
00:00:32.183 --> 00:00:35.021
Your turn. Say: Democracy.

10
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:38.972
Democracy.

11
00:00:39.717 --> 00:00:40.834
Governance.

12
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.125
Governance means the processes by which institutions are directed and held accountable.

13
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:48.995
Weak governance encourages corruption.

14
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:52.166
Your turn. Say: Governance.

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.013
Governance.

16
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.007
Legislation.

17
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:02.061
Legislation means laws enacted by a parliament.

18
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.787
The legislation was passed despite strong opposition.

19
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:08.966
Your turn. Say: Legislation.

20
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.010
Legislation.

21
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:14.902
Sovereignty.

22
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:20.145
Sovereignty means supreme authority of a state over its own affairs.

23
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.388
Trade agreements can limit national sovereignty.

24
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.462
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

25
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.301
Sovereignty.

26
00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.318
Constitution.

27
00:01:33.317 --> 00:01:37.255
Constitution means the fundamental principles by which a state is governed.

28
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.662
The constitution guarantees freedom of expression.

29
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.995
Your turn. Say: Constitution.

30
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:47.985
Constitution.

31
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.123
Policy-making.

32
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:54.672
Policy-making means the process of designing public decisions.

33
00:01:55.283 --> 00:01:57.678
Evidence should inform policy-making.

34
00:01:58.283 --> 00:02:01.133
Your turn. Say: Policy-making.

35
00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:05.479
Policy-making.

36
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:07.589
Representation.

37
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:12.719
Representation means the extent to which groups have a voice in decision-making.

38
00:02:13.317 --> 00:02:16.199
Women remain underrepresented in many parliaments.

39
00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:19.830
Your turn. Say: Representation.

40
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:24.004
Representation.

41
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:25.972
Civic duty.

42
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:30.980
Civic duty means the responsibilities of a citizen to society.

43
00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:34.396
Jury service is regarded as a civic duty.

44
00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:37.601
Your turn. Say: Civic duty.

45
00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:41.630
Civic duty.

46
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:43.559
Bureaucracy.

47
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.999
Bureaucracy means an administrative system with complex rules and procedures.

48
00:02:49.583 --> 00:02:52.163
Excessive bureaucracy delayed the reforms.

49
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.530
Your turn. Say: Bureaucracy.

50
00:02:58.367 --> 00:02:59.565
Bureaucracy.

51
00:03:00.317 --> 00:03:01.387
Federalism.

52
00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:07.042
Federalism means a system dividing power between central and regional governments.

53
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.078
Federalism allows regions to tailor education policy.

54
00:03:11.683 --> 00:03:14.220
Your turn. Say: Federalism.

55
00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:17.998
Federalism.

56
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.439
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:23.283 --> 00:03:28.641
Visal, the new transport policy has been widely criticised. What went wrong, in your view?

58
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:36.884
The policy should have considered representation more carefully. Rural communities were barely consulted.

59
00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:40.436
Could the government have avoided the protests?

60
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:48.370
It could have held public hearings before the legislation was drafted. That might have built trust.

61
00:03:49.483 --> 00:03:52.516
Some say the protests were exaggerated by the media.

62
00:03:53.617 --> 00:03:59.889
That may have played a role, but the government must have known that ignoring rural voters would backfire.

63
00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:02.545
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:03.383 --> 00:04:05.511
Grammar focus: Modal perfect.

65
00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:07.102
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:07.817 --> 00:04:11.349
The policy should have considered representation more carefully.

67
00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.958
The government could have held public hearings first.

68
00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.012
Officials must have known the risks.

69
00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:27.387
Modal perfects judge the past: 'should have' criticises, 'could have' shows an unused option, 'might have' speculates, and 'must have' expresses a confident deduction.

70
00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:32.172
Be careful! Don't say: They should had consulted the public.

71
00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:36.841
Say: They should have consulted the public.

72
00:04:37.141 --> 00:04:40.824
Modal perfect: modal + have + past participle.

73
00:04:41.633 --> 00:04:44.286
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.740
When Policy Fails.

75
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.197
Democracy depends not only on elections but also on transparent governance between them.

76
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:59.809
When a policy provokes widespread protest, analysts ask what decision-makers should have done differently.

77
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:07.594
In many cases, the policy should have considered representation more carefully, especially for marginalised groups.

78
00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:14.535
Public consultation could have revealed problems early, while excessive bureaucracy may have slowed the response.

79
00:05:15.533 --> 00:05:20.354
Such failures remind citizens that participation is a civic duty, not a privilege.

80
00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:25.376
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:26.217 --> 00:05:29.435
The policy should blank representation more carefully.

82
00:05:29.735 --> 00:05:33.981
A, have considered. B, had considered. C, considered.

83
00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:39.475
The answer is A: have considered.

84
00:05:40.617 --> 00:05:44.929
'I'm almost certain they knew the risks.' → They blank the risks.

85
00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:49.277
A, should have known. B, must have known. C, could know.

86
00:05:52.833 --> 00:05:55.239
The answer is B: must have known.

87
00:05:56.383 --> 00:06:00.809
Which modal perfect describes an option that was available but not taken?

88
00:06:01.109 --> 00:06:04.798
A, could have. B, must have. C, will have.

89
00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.031
The answer is A: could have.

90
00:06:11.183 --> 00:06:13.636
The blank guarantees freedom of expression.

91
00:06:13.936 --> 00:06:18.286
A, bureaucracy. B, constitution. C, sovereignty.

92
00:06:21.833 --> 00:06:23.972
The answer is B: constitution.

93
00:06:25.117 --> 00:06:28.591
According to the reading, what could public consultation have done?

94
00:06:28.891 --> 00:06:34.669
A, revealed problems early. B, removed bureaucracy. C, replaced elections.

95
00:06:38.233 --> 00:06:41.371
The answer is A: revealed problems early.

96
00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:45.823
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:06:46.683 --> 00:06:49.321
What makes a political system fair and effective?

98
00:07:06.117 --> 00:07:07.512
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:07.812 --> 00:07:39.382
I believe a political system is fair when every group has genuine representation, not just the majority. It is effective when laws are made transparently and leaders can be held accountable. For instance, many failed policies should have involved local communities from the start. Some argue that strong, centralised leadership is more efficient, and in a crisis that may be true. In the long run, however, fairness and effectiveness depend on each other.

100
00:07:39.682 --> 00:07:41.867
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:07:42.783 --> 00:07:46.771
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and modal perfect.

102
00:07:47.071 --> 00:07:52.893
Today's value is civic responsibility. Take care of your city, because it is everyone's home.

103
00:07:53.193 --> 00:07:55.706
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

104
00:07:56.006 --> 00:07:58.495
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

